#: admin/views/settings.php:151
msgid "Paste one of these into a page to show that pipeline's submissions. Anyone who can open the page can read the table, so treat the token as a password. By default the table shows the date, name and stage, never the email address. A submission without a name shows as \"(no name)\" rather than its address. Add columns=\"submitted_at,name,stage,email\" only if you really want addresses on a public page. The owner and tags columns are public too once you add them, so leave out internal tags. The Submissions Table block uses the same defaults. If EntryVault is ever deactivated, WordPress prints an unknown shortcode as plain text, token included; the block does not, so prefer the block on public pages."
